course: dictionary
lesson: 6389
title: A multitude of meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Multitude means a very large number of things or people. In «a multitude of reasons», the word tells you there are lots of reasons, not just one or two.
explain: Look at the clues around it. In «a multitude of colours», the colours are many and varied, so multitude means a big number. Try your meaning in the sentence: "a very large number of colours" still makes sense, so you have it.
explain: In old Bible stories, the multitude means a huge crowd of people. Jesus fed the multitude with five loaves and two fish. In «Love covers over a multitude of sins», the word means "lots of", and the sentence says love hides many faults.
fact: Multitude means a very large number of things or people.
fact: «A multitude of reasons» means many reasons, and «a multitude of colours» means many colours.
fact: In Bible stories, «the multitude» means a huge crowd of people.
fact: «Cover a multitude of sins» means hide many faults.
fact: Multitude is a noun, and its plural is multitudes.
exercise: «She had a multitude of reasons for being late.» What does multitude mean here? | A kind friend | Being late for hours | *A very large number | A single, small reason
exercise: Jesus fed the multitude with five loaves and two fish. What is the multitude? | A basket of bread | *A huge crowd of people | A kind of fish | A small group of friends
exercise: In «a multitude of colours», which word shows there are lots of colours? | a | *multitude | of | colours
test: «The sky was filled with a multitude of stars.» What does multitude mean? | Cloudy | Tiny | *A very large number | Very cold
test: In «Love covers over a multitude of sins», what does multitude suggest? | A crowd in a church | A single small fault | Lost money | *Many faults
exam: A student says «multitude» means a small number. Is the student right? | Yes, it means a small number | Only when talking about two people | It means the same as few | *No, it means a very large number
